===Glyph origin===
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|士|呂|今|alt3=亼|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=s|c3=p|t1=[[upside-down]] ritual [[axe]] perhaps made of precious materials|t2=two [[block]]s of [[metal]]}}. The semantic component in folk etymology is very often misinterpreted as {{och-l|*王}}. The blocks of metal were originally drawn as ''':''' and then as {{och-l|*丷}}.

The upper component {{lang-lite|zh|sc=Hani|亼}} is the ancient form for {{och-l|今}} and it represents an open mouth flipped upside down; it indicates the original pronunciation of the whole character.

Shuowen interprets it as an image of metal in the [[earth]] (Xu Shen writes "象金在土中形") with {{lang-lite|zh|sc=Hani|亼}} as phonetic. But the most ancient versions do not contain the pictogram of the earth.

Originally referred to [[copper]], first used during the Copper Age and the Jade Age in China (hence before the Bronze Age, during which the first bronzes were produced); later the meaning was extended to [[metal]] as a whole, and then to [[gold]].

===Etymology===
{{zh-ref|Starostin}} reconstructs {{inh|zh|sit-pro|*gǝ̆m||[[metal]]}}; compare {{cog-lite|ctd|xam||[[gold]]}} and {{cog|lep|ᰀᰩᰮ|tr=kóm|t=[[silver]], [[coin]], [[money]]}}.

“[[copper|Copper]]” {{defdate|prior to 5th {{BCE}}}} → “metals in general” → “gold”.

The main senses now are “gold” and “metal”.

Some of the Manchu people with this surname are descendants of the [[Qing]] imperial family and is a {{calque|zh|mnc|ᠠᡳᠰᡳᠨ|nocap=1|t=gold}} in their original surname {{l|mnc|ᠠᡳᠰᡳᠨ ᡤᡳᠣᡵᠣ}}.

